            <ArticleTitle>A U-shaped association of body mass index and all-cause mortality in heart failure patients: A dose-response meta-analysis of prospective cohort studies.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/1755-5922.12232</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The role of body mass index (BMI) for the prognosis of heart failure (HF) patients remains to be a great interest for a long time. However, the precise effect of dose-response for BMI and mortality risk in patients with HF is still unclear. We conducted a dose-response meta-analysis to quantitatively assess the effect of BMI on all-cause mortality in subjects with HF.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Fourteen prospective cohort studies included 13 508 death cases identified among 46 794 patients with HF.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The summary HR per 5 units increase in BMI was 0.95 (95% CI=0.92-0.97) with evidence of high heterogeneity (I(2) =90.10%, Pheterogeneity &lt;.00001), and an U-shaped nonlinear relationship of BMI and risk of mortality from all-causes was revealed (Pnonlinearity =.0025). When stratified analyses were conducted, the heterogeneity in patients with HF aged more than 60 years decreased.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Heart failure patients with higher BMI (&gt;28 kg/m(2) ) have a better survival, whereas underweight and those with severe obesity (eg, BMI&gt;37 kg/m(2) ) have a higher mortality.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2016 John Wiley &amp; Sons Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
